<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Girsu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Telloh, Tello</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Girsu was a Sumerian city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, at the mound now called Telloh, and the religious and political capital of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> for much of the 3rd millennium BCE. It was the cult centre of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a>, whose temple Eninnu was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> around 2100 BCE. Excavations there from 1877 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-de-sarzec">Ernest de Sarzec</a> first revealed Sumerian civilisation to modern scholars.</p></div>
